{
  "description": "Which era a server speaks, from the answer to Strata's `server/discover` probe (MCP 2026-07-28, \"Backward Compatibility\" in the stdio and Streamable HTTP transports; design/50-MCP-Client.md, \"Opening a connection\"), replayed by both engines' era tests. Strata's modern version is `2026-07-28`. Its legacy versions, newest first, are `2025-11-25`, `2025-06-18`, `2025-03-26`, and on stdio only `2024-11-05`. The recognized modern errors are `-32020` (HeaderMismatch), `-32021` (MissingRequiredClientCapability) and `-32022` (UnsupportedProtocolVersion). A stdio `answer` is a JSON-RPC `result`, an `error`, or `silence` (no answer within the probe timeout). An HTTP `answer` is a status, a content type and a body. `outcome.era` is `modern` (with the version used from then on), `legacy` (with the version Strata sends in `initialize`: the newest legacy version it knows that the server listed, else `2025-11-25`), or `fail` (with words the error message `contains`). The rule: a `DiscoverResult` is modern when it lists `2026-07-28`, else legacy with a version it lists, else a failure naming both lists. `-32022` is modern but not this version: legacy with a version from `data.supported`, else a failure naming both lists. `-32020` and `-32021` fail, with their code. On stdio, any other error, and silence, are legacy. On HTTP, a 2xx body is a JSON-RPC message (JSON, or the last event of an SSE stream) read as on stdio, except that silence can't happen; 401 and 403 fail as unauthorized, with the status; 5xx fail, with the status; any other 4xx is legacy, unless its body is a JSON-RPC message with a recognized modern error, which is read as on stdio.",
